# <<[ package_name ]>>-http

## Purpose

Owns only the public Axum and HTTP boundary for `<<[ repo_name ]>>`: routes, handlers, extractors, HTTP DTOs, and the public OpenAPI document.

## Key entrypoints

- `src/lib.rs`: `assemble` builds the public surface through Batter's `HttpBoundary`; `in_process` serves tests; `public_openapi` exports the contract.
- `src/public.rs`: owns public routes, HTTP DTOs, OpenAPI operations, and probe documentation.

## Edit here for X

- Add business routes to `application_routes` with `#[utoipa::path]`. The boundary admits exactly the documented paths, so an undocumented route is never served.
- Read the request's operation context and correlation ID with the `AdmittedRequest` extractor; pass `admitted.context()` to nested operations.
- Call app-crate use cases through `AppState` instead of placing business logic in handlers.
- Regenerate committed contracts and clients with the root `contract:generate` package script.

## Invariants

- Keep handlers thin and delegate domain work to `<<[ package_name ]>>`.
- Production entrypoints use `assemble` with the runtime's operation admission and readiness policy. `in_process` uses an independent, approved lifecycle for tests and proves response construction only.
- `HttpBoundary` owns the layer order: correlation and the single HTTP observer outermost, probes outside admission, and admission plus the request deadline around every route and the fallback. Do not compose `batter::axum::low_level` middleware here.
- Request IDs are server-generated; inbound `x-request-id` headers are replaced and never used as identity.
- `/health`, `/health/live`, and `/health/ready` are rendered by the boundary with the shared probe renderers. Keep their documentation in `probe_documentation` aligned; a test checks that documented paths equal served routes plus probes.
- Readiness adds the `application-state` condition to the runtime's readiness policy. Add further conditions with `with_condition`; they run synchronously and must not block.
- Never depend on `<<[ package_name ]>>-admin-http` or mount privileged routes here. Admin transport has a separate crate and binary deployment boundary.
- Return JSON errors through `<<[ package_name ]>>-http-common`; never disclose internal error chains.

## Common commands

- `cargo test -p <<[ package_name ]>>-http`
- `cargo test -p <<[ package_name ]>>-test-support --test http`
